N5008 [basic.type.qualifier], [dcl.fct]/6-7: cv-qualifiers nested inside a
function type's parameter types (`void(*)(const int*)` vs `void(*)(int*)`)
and the cv-qualifier-seq of a member function type (`int (S::*)(int) const`
vs `int (S::*)(int)`, modelled as a const implicit object parameter) do not
make the types distinct for std::is_same / `<T, T>` deduction: type identity
compares irep structure, and cv-qualifiers are `#`-attributes it ignores.
Top-level cv (`const int*` vs `int*`) IS distinguished.  Pre-existing; found
while fixing the alias-declaration spelling of cv-qualified pointer-to-member
function types (cpp11_alias_pointer_to_member_function_cv).
Fixed 2026-09-23: qualifiers_match_recursively (partial-specialization
matching, `is_same<T, T>`) and the `__is_same` builtin now compare the
return and parameter types of function types, so the cv-qualifiers nested
there -- and a member function's cv-qualifier-seq -- distinguish the types.
The typedef declarator spelling `typedef int (S::*T)(int) const;' lost the
cv-qualifier-seq (cpp_declarator_convertert now attaches it to the pointed-to
function type as Parser::rTypeName does for a type-id).
